**Chapter 9, 52: Aldebaran I**

"—No one can defeat the you I created."

Aldebaran could remember the Witch saying those words to him as if it were yesterday.

In real time, it was nearly twenty years ago; in terms of subjective time, it was far, far longer than that. He remembered the number of retries for individual situations, but he didn't remember the total count of all his challenges, and he didn't even want to think about the staggering totality of all the time he had spent. But it was absolutely certain that he had spent far more time than a single, normal life of living and dying.

That said, even after spending that much time, it wasn't as if Aldebaran's spirit had reached the same state as an Elf who had lived agelessly for centuries.

If the human spirit matures from immaturity, eventually reaching a state of seasoned equanimity, it's because the time spent allowed it to experience both bitterness and sweetness, giving the spirit room to grow.

But the time Aldebaran spent, beyond real time, was a continuous series of stagnant moments, so to speak, all for the sake of breaking through the obstacle lying before him. In reality, it was just countless repetitions of mere seconds, or at most minutes. No matter how many times you repeat just that much time, no growth can be expected for a human.

Therefore, Aldebaran considered the accumulation of time repeated by his Authority to be futile time that only wore down his heart, not fostering growth.

Even with such futile time, after repeating it countless times—thousands, tens of thousands, hundreds of millions of times—the brain naturally organizes memories, trying to steal away Aldebaran's genuine memories.

Even so, there certainly were memories that refused to fade, memories that felt etched not into his brain but into his very soul. The words at the beginning were precisely that.

Though, if Aldebaran were to say that he didn't remember all that time, and that he didn't even want to remember it, he could vividly picture the Witch—the very incarnation of curiosity who wanted to know everything—muttering, "Is that so? What a shame."

The Witch, who pretended not to understand emotions, was skilled at acting to manipulate others as she pleased. Even knowing that, Aldebaran couldn't defy the Witch's words or attitude.

He had been trained and remade that way. —Saying it like that, it might be a close match to the inhumanity of the Shinobi Village he'd only heard snippets about from Yae.

In this case, was it that the Shinobi Village, being treated on par with the Witch, was insane, or that the Witch, who could single-handedly match the history of the Shinobi Village, was too dangerous? It was probably six of one, half a dozen of the other.

—Anyway, the current Aldebaran was a thing created by the Witch.

"Even so, you have the right to live your own life. That alone is a domain that should not be unilaterally violated, even by your creator. So, if you truly feel in your heart that you cannot follow me, you may leave the plan at any time. That is your freedom."

On occasion, the Witch would say such things, as if testing Aldebaran's resolve.

It was an unusual consideration for the Witch. Basically, once she obtained the other party's consent, her usual strategy was to maneuver so that the promise given couldn't be taken back.

In fact, among the lessons and tasks imposed on Aldebaran by the Witch, there were many nightmarish contents that he would have balked at if he hadn't been made to promise not to give up first. When making him do those things, the Witch, after getting his initial nod, never asked if he wanted to stop.

Yet, regarding the fundamental, original, initial resolve, she questioned him about it frequently.

Aldebaran interpreted that as perhaps being the Witch's own form of guilt or conflict, but what did it really mean?

The Witch, who claimed not to understand emotions, surely still knew anger, sadness, and joy. If she could express such emotions, couldn't she also feel guilt or fear of the future?

At the very least, during the time Aldebaran spent with the Witch, while there were times he thought she didn't understand the human heart, he never thought she lacked a human heart.

Between 'lacking' and 'not understanding', entirely different landscapes spread out.

Perhaps that difference manifested in the form of the Witch's questions. If so, did the Witch hesitate each time she asked those questions?
Was there conflict within her, worrying about the harsh path Aldebaran would have to walk, and trying to respect Aldebaran's own will, to let him choose that life for himself?

If, if, by some million-to-one, billion-to-one chance, the Witch had such feelings—then, ironically, that very thing would become the reason Aldebaran would not waver in his resolve.

Therefore—,

"—I see."

To Aldebaran's always identical reply, the Witch's reaction was also always the same.

Perhaps he was being deceived.

From Aldebaran's birth until that day, the Witch might have spent ample time brainwashing him. Perhaps the Witch didn't possess any of the guilt or hesitation that Aldebaran hoped for.

Perhaps she was just that kind of... bad Witch.

But, even if he asked her that directly, the Witch would float that beautiful black and white smile of hers and,

"Of course. —I'm a bad Magician, you know."

With that, she would torment Aldebaran with an answer that he couldn't tell was sincere or a lie.

