Roaring wind battered him as Aldebaran found himself in a high-speed glide.

He thought the sight of himself flying with stone-made wings spread on his back, catching the wind, must be terribly ridiculous, but there was no time to worry about appearances.

While Yae was buying time, he had to gain as much distance as possible and eliminate the altitude difference.

"Can't imagine they purposely sniped my 'Territory', though..."

Aldebaran grumbled, but the majority of his consciousness was devoted to the formation and control of the stone wings on his back.

Forming the stone wings required a delicate balance, needing fine adjustments in both size and hardness. If they were too large, they'd be tossed about too much by the wind; if too small, they couldn't serve their purpose. The strength and density of the hardness also related to weight; shaving off too much or leaving too much would both lead to an unavoidable crash.

"I respect Icarus for succeeding on his first try...!"

Speaking of flying through the sky with artificial wings brought to mind the great predecessor Icarus with his wax wings. Regardless of his fate, one had to honestly marvel that he not only glided with artificial wings but even flapped them to approach the sun.

As for Aldebaran, how many times had he crashed due to his imperfect wings? —Realizing that was playing right into the enemy's hands, after numerous retries, he had finally managed the current stone wings.

Even these were a desperate measure that couldn't be sustained without extreme concentration and meticulous care—,

"Couldn't just keep falling like that."

That fact and his sense of urgency were the biggest reasons Aldebaran had frantically leveled up his stone wings from level 1 to at least a flyable level. Though Aldebaran prided himself on having an invincible Authority, his 'Territory' wasn't without weaknesses.

The enemy's surprise attack had, unintentionally, simultaneously targeted two of those weaknesses. Strictly speaking, perhaps it was attacking the same weakness from two angles.

Aldebaran's Authority, the 'Territory', had an effective range.

The teleportation brought about by the surprise attack had thrown Aldebaran outside the set area of his 'Territory'. On top of that, the teleportation destination included a fall from an extreme altitude, and this became the second method of 'Territory' destruction that assailed Aldebaran.

As a result, while falling at tremendous speed, Aldebaran repeatedly killed himself before flying out of his 'Territory', and restarted again from mid-fall. During those repetitions, he judged it impossible to break the situation while falling from an uncontrollable extreme altitude. After trying various other measures from there, he eventually arrived at attempting escape with stone wings.

At the very least, this slowed the rate of altitude change, making it relatively easier to maintain his 'Territory'. Just as Aldebaran managed to untangle one stubborn knot in this way—,

"—Al!!"

A silvery voice sharply called out to Aldebaran, who was desperately trying to stabilize his body.

Glancing back with just his head, he could clearly see the figure of an angel—no, Emilia, flying through the air from a much lower altitude than him, but heading in the same direction.

On her slender back, she bore wings made of ice. Though using the same method as Aldebaran, the difference in appearance was overwhelming, and she was rapidly closing the gap.

"Unbelievable."

He muttered, dumbfounded, almost impressed. He wanted them to understand it wasn't without reason.

As mentioned before, Aldebaran had struggled greatly to complete these stone wings, however clumsy they were. It was precisely because of that struggle that he felt respect for Icarus of the wax wings.

But, sensing the same talent in Emilia—who, like the mythical Icarus, succeeded with her artificial wings on the first try and achieved the same result as Aldebaran who had failed countless times—he felt not respect, but a decisive gap, even a sense of defeat.

"It's no consolation that the line where I, who crashed over six hundred times, just suck is better than the line where the young lady is a genius."

The strength of Aldebaran's 'Territory' lay in its endurance, the ability to persist through infinite trials until even a single fragment of a chance for victory arrived. Its greatest advantage was being able to keep drawing cards until the opponent failed and a situation advantageous to Aldebaran arrived.

However, as his fight with Reinhard had shown, the world occasionally contained beings who always drew the correct answer. That 'correct answer' varied depending on the individual's abilities and victory conditions, but what they shared in common, unlike Aldebaran, was being loved by fate.

—Aldebaran was hated by fate.

Whether Aldebaran hated it first, or fate hated Aldebaran first, was no longer knowable. But that alone was an unshakeable fact. Regardless of who else loved or hated him, fate was Aldebaran's absolute enemy.

That's why he had to kill it. —Even if it meant making the world his enemy, he would kill fate.

Not wanting that to happen, fate sent its darlings, one after another, as assassins. Here and now, Emilia and Rem were exactly that.

"—. What a troublesome thing."

Just who was it that suggested choosing Rem for the surprise attack role initially?

Whoever proposed it, she was the most suitable of the suitable for creating an opening in Aldebaran. When she was a victim of the 'Gluttony' Authority, Aldebaran, under its influence, couldn't perceive the abnormality of Rem's existence, and unintentionally built a proper relationship with her.

Therefore, during that moment of the flash grenade memory—when Priscilla died and he learned Rem was also freed from the yoke of 'Gluttony'—he was hit with a larger void than anyone else.

—Because he had encountered a being who shouldn't be here, who should originally be dead.

"————"

There was no room for debate about what method had brought about this result.

Undoubtedly, he had done it. Natsuki Subaru had resisted fate, had triumphed over fate. Natsuki Subaru possessed that power. The proof was Rem's existence.

But, even so—he didn't save Priscilla Barielle.

"—If you're gonna do that, then I ain't holdin' back either."

The tears shed, the fists that hurt himself, the considerate attempts to empathize... none of it had meaning.

What he wanted wasn't tears, wounds, or comfort. It was just one single life.

"—Tch!"

Gnashing his teeth hard enough to taste blood inside his helmet, Aldebaran turned to look behind him. Simultaneously, he transformed the angle and thickness of his wings, minimizing the loss of speed and maneuverability, and locked eyes with Emilia in mid-air as she chased after him.

Emilia looked very fitting with her beautiful ice wings, but no matter how angelically lovely she was, she couldn't gain speed or altitude by flapping her artificial wings. Her initial velocity came from the recoil of Rem punching the ice sheet, but her subsequent pursuit used a different method—,

"Ha! Ta! Tei!"

Her shout when attacking was just as devoid of intensity, but with each cry, her speed and altitude increased by one level, then another. The method was simple and clear—she used magic to create pillars of ice in the air as footholds, and kept kicking off them to gain acceleration and momentum.

The created ice pillars, after a moment's停滞, were kicked down towards the ground by Emilia's leg strength, but they would quickly disperse into mana and return to the atmosphere, being reborn as new footholds. She'd kick off those again for more momentum. By repeating this, she had been chasing after Aldebaran.

"This is ridiculous...!"

It was a brute force solution only possible with her exceptional magic sense and the ability to wield vast amounts of mana.

Thanks to his pact with 'Aldebaran', Aldebaran could borrow the power of the 'Divine Dragon', an external mana tank, but even if told to do the same as Emilia, he absolutely couldn't.

It wasn't a problem of mana capacity, but the gap in sense and physical ability was too great. —The sense gap might be bridgeable through infinite trials, but the physical ability gap was not.

The ravine continued long and vast, the rock walls enclosing the two fliers on left and right stretching just as far. As long as they were heading the same direction, Emilia's pursuit wouldn't cease.

Seeing that Aldebaran had understood this, Emilia kicked off another new ice pillar. Though there was still distance, she matched his altitude. Then, while balancing with her ice wings—,

"—Al! Please, let's talk! I'll listen properly!"
"You've got some nerve. After going 'no questions asked' and trying to freeze me solid without holding back. If I'd kept falling all the way to the bottom, you were planning to freeze the whole river and call it a day, right?"
"But! That plan already failed!"
"Being too honest is something you should think about, Royal Selection candidate!"

Staying put and falling all the way to the bottom was a pattern that would have meant completely falling into the enemy's trap.

To escape that, he had chosen the risky path of deviating from the ice sheet's falling trajectory. But perhaps he should consider it a success that he managed to separate Emilia and Rem, even if not both at once.

The answer to that was—,

"I'm showin' you now. —My attitude hasn't changed from back in the royal capital!"

Raising his hand, intending to shake off the pursuing Emilia, Aldebaran created stone projectiles in the air, aiming to make her charge into a barrage of flying rocks.

Unexpectedly, a scene reminiscent of a real shooting game unfolded. As Emilia was met with the barrage of stone projectiles, her ice wings glinting, she cried out loudly,

"And I'll say it as many times as it takes! I don't want to have to think that *anyone* is gone and there's nothing I can do about it and cry anymore!!"

With that loud plea, Emilia plunged into the barrage. The sound of shattering ice and the scream of freezing air echoed sharply through Agzdad Ravine.

**--Aldebaran had never really agonized over the meaning of his existence as the 'Aftercoming Star'.**

It seems everyone goes through a phase during adolescence where they wonder why they were born, where life comes from and where it goes, or feel a sort of unreasoning rebelliousness towards their parents, but none of these were particularly relevant propositions for Aldebaran.

In Aldebaran's case, it was difficult to decide who to set as the parent he should rebel against, and due to the nature of his Authority, he couldn't even grasp the reality of a lost life truly disappearing. As for the purpose of his birth, he had been given a clear answer, and regarding *why* he was born, he thought he could probably answer 'Love'.

So, Aldebaran never had to confront the propositions typical of adolescence.

He didn't consider this a failure or a missed opportunity.

There seems to be an idea in the world that one should seek out hardship while young, but there's probably no value in experiences that you'd later look back on as embarrassing 'black history' as an adult. It's fine not to have experiences you don't need to have. --Just like how everyone should only die once, at the very end of their life.

"I see. So I cannot expect adolescent-like emotions or impulses from you. That is somewhat disappointing. I had been thinking of various ways to handle it when you reached your rebellious phase."

That was the lament of the Witch, who often made heartless comments to Aldebaran, who grew up straightforwardly without causing trouble, though he didn't think she was completely heartless.

For Aldebaran, the Witch was the one he had spent the most time with since his birth, but she had a bad habit of wanting to witness every human choice and emotion, every one of the myriad possibilities.

Furthermore, in most cases, being unable to put a brake on these desires was part of the Witch's very nature.

Aldebaran was acquainted with other Witches besides her, and while the degree varied, all of them were unable to defy that nature, which felt terribly blasphemous to him.

Therefore, if he thought of the Witch's attitude—waiting unsatisfied for Aldebaran's adolescence to arrive—as something brought about by an unconquerable nature, there was an aspect of it being unavoidable. That said, it made no sense to him to *perform* adolescence just to please the Witch. --In a way, he felt that very sense of rebellion might itself be an adolescent-like emotion.

"As I thought, it's too convenient for me, who isn't even your parent, to try and superficially trace a parent-like experience... I wonder if I could have experienced it if I had created Beatrice."

The loneliness Aldebaran felt when the Witch muttered such questions to herself was, he thought, unrelated to adolescence, something else entirely. But it was difficult to seek out what that was in a concrete form; their relationship was too complex for that.

So, just as the Witch had decreed, the relationship between Aldebaran and her was—

"—Sensei."

When he called out like that, the Witch, who had been enjoying her exploration in the labyrinth of thought, lifted her face.

Whenever he saw her black eyes reflect him and her head tilt with a "What is it?", her pure white hair cascading over her shoulders, he couldn't help but think what a cunning Witch she was.

She was always absorbed in her own affairs, honest about her desires, and never made any attempt to consider others' feelings, yet whenever Aldebaran called out, she would always meet his eyes.

It was only natural that he didn't feel adolescent rebelliousness towards that Witch.

The motivation was thin to begin with, and his mind and body had long since yielded to the futility of opposing the Witch. Under the guise of performance experiments for mastering his Authority, the Witch had subjected Aldebaran to over a million 'deaths'—the ranking was already settled.

Though, no matter how much he talked about that, the Witch in question would just give him the cold shoulder, saying, "Since not a single one remains in my memory, it's troublesome to be told to feel guilty."

Anyway, he understood the reason the Witch went that far.

After all, by that time, among the surviving Witches, aside from the one before him, there was only one other who was the direct or indirect cause of death for the other Witches. Aldebaran's points of contact with the other Witches weren't with them during their lifetimes either.

He could only meet them in the limited spaces prepared by the Witch. The 'them' he met there were already deceased, existences of only collected souls.

And under the Witch's tutelage, Aldebaran had to—

"—kill that one person."

That was the meaning of existence given to Aldebaran, the 'Aftercoming Star', even before he could reach adolescence. It was the guiding principle of his life that he had to fulfill, even if it meant dying a million times.

To make Aldebaran into this, the Witch was criticized by the other Witches and cut ties with her comrades, so her resolve was absolute.

"—No one can defeat the you I created."

Being told that was Aldebaran's pride and the basis of his existence.

Honestly, he thought the Witch's personality was the worst, and there were many things he wanted to say about her methods of training and presenting her plans, but even so, he never hesitated to meet the Witch's expectations.

He believed he could.

Back then, when Aldebaran still had his left arm, even though he knew 'Death', he didn't know true defeat, so he had been able to believe that.

He never had an adolescence—but it was unmistakably a foolish 'black history', so indelible it felt like it could never be erased.

