"Tch—!"

As Emilia plunged headlong into the hail of stone projectiles, Aldebaran suppressed the hesitation in his heart and poured on the firepower without holding back.

Just like during their clash in the royal capital, Aldebaran was instinctively weak against Emilia.

This was, by now, a sort of fundamental weakness inherent to life itself, which was also why, back in the royal capital, he couldn't bring himself to take the drastic measures he might have used against other opponents.

Back then, he had borrowed 'Aldebaran's' power to drop a massive boulder, stopping Emilia's pursuit in exchange for protecting the capital, but here, there was nothing he could use as collateral damage.

In that sense too, dropping them into this massive ravine showed that the lessons from their last fight had been properly considered.

"I ain't got enough leeway to appreciate that growth of yours!"

Unfortunately, Aldebaran's heart wasn't generous enough to rejoice in his own or his enemy's growth. For a moment, he felt resistance to defining Emilia as an enemy, but he shut his eyes tightly against it.

That resolve was something he had made long, long ago, even before meeting Priscilla.

Strictly speaking, even if the target of Aldebaran's resolve wasn't the Emilia before him—

"Even a recycled resolve can work as a stopgap!"

Brandishing that self-aware, stopgap resolve, he set about cutting off Emilia's pursuit.

Straight on, Emilia flew majestically into the storm of fist-sized rock fragments, her ice wings glinting.

It was an attack launched in the hope that she would slow down even a little by taking an evasive or defensive posture, but just as his bad预感 predicted, Emilia went for a straight, shortest-route confrontation—firing ice pillars for footholds from her back's ice wings, and even launching ice spikes to counter the barrage, while using twin ice swords gripped in both hands to bat away any projectiles that made it through the interception.

He had been taught by the Witch that simultaneous activation of multiple spells was difficult even for seasoned mages.

Even with the same attribute, it required the skill to perform a nearly impossible task, like holding a pen in each hand and writing an essay with the right while drawing a picture with the left. If they were different attributes, it was apparently a desperate measure akin to adding extra brains for the purpose. Aldebaran had once seen the Witch handling five attributes simultaneously. He had genuinely wondered if her slightly smaller-than-average head contained five tiny brains.

Even setting aside such exceptional cases, simultaneously activating four different types of magic—wings, ice pillars, ice spikes, and twin swords, all of the same ice attribute—was a sufficiently impressive feat in itself.

Add to that the fact that she was employing an absurd pursuit technique, demonstrating athletic prowess surpassing an Olympic athlete, and he felt he had already used up his daily quota for surprises.

"That's why I'm welcoming you with a low shock factor!"

In response to the storm of projectiles, Emilia chose to break through with courage and physical strength. But courage aside, what if he presented a threat that couldn't be overcome by physical strength alone?

"————"

Emilia's face tightened as she, eager to close the distance, found the next phase of his attack prepared before her.

Spreading out in front of her, inescapably and all at once, was a包围网 of sand and grit, even smaller than the rock fragments, whose damage was easy to overlook. It was akin to plunging into a downpour; unless she possessed the absurdity to dodge rain in mid-air, taking zero hits was impossible.

Of course, his aim wasn't just to blind her by scattering sand—

"—Huh, it's heavy!?"

Emilia cried out in surprise as she tried to shake off the clinging sand.

The sand grains that touched her limbs and white clothing coalesced with the surrounding grains, hindering her movement with their increased weight and hardness. It was a debuff attack he had used on Reinhard in the sea of sand—Reinhard, moving at supersonic speeds, had torn it off through sheer momentum, but that method was impossible for Emilia too.

Furthermore—

"Her wings—"

The primary target of the grit was to disrupt the ice wings supporting Emilia's flight.

The ice wings, having flown into the dust cloud, were eroded by the sand, unable to perform their original function of catching the wind. Losing her balance, Emilia began to drop altitude sharply, heading straight down towards the ground headfirst—to avoid that, she immediately discarded the hampered wings, reverting from an angel back to an angelic girl to escape the peril.

"—She jettisoned them!"

But forcing her to that point was Aldebaran's real aim.

Even if she discarded the ice wings once, if it were Emilia, she could redeploy them instantly and restart without losing her upward and forward momentum. However, no matter how exceptional her physical abilities, she couldn't catch up to an impromptu hang glider exceeding a hundred kilometers per hour.

Therefore, Aldebaran pressed the attack to prevent Emilia from redeploying her wings.

With a roar, thrusting out from the rock wall came a giant stone arm attempting to grab Emilia as she flew through the ravine's airspace from the side. If it could kill her momentum, then even if Emilia obtained a second set of wings, she wouldn't be able to catch up to Aldebaran, whose speed wouldn't die.

Moreover, it wasn't just one or two giant arms blocking Emilia's path. —The stone arms protruding from the rock wall as their material numbered close to a hundred.

"Respect to the Thousand-Armed Kannon... or more like, feels like the backstage of a haunted house."

The imagery was close to that classic haunted house scene where hands suddenly shoot out all at once from the *shoji* screens. However, unlike those meant for scaring, these giant arms were seriously trying to capture Emilia.

If even one managed to grab her leg, this chase would be completely over—it was the critical moment for Aldebaran's successful escape—

"Can you get past thi—" "—I'll do my best!!"

His words, about to ask if she could overcome it, were overwritten by her sheer determination.

He didn't think sheer effort could solve this, but the fact that she seemed likely to manage *somehow* through sheer effort was also what made Emilia terrifying.

Stripped of her wings, Emilia displayed that terror to its fullest—spinning the ice sword in her hand, she sliced off the fingers of the first giant arm, then used the now-harmless arm as a foothold to leap high.

Her momentum wasn't dead. She was also regaining a lot of altitude.

But, however, even if the arms weren't a thousand, there were still over a hundred more lying in wait—

"—Soldier-san, lend me your strength!"

Answering Emilia's call, the ice soldiers with their detestable appearance appeared once more.

The ice soldiers, who should have been helplessly shattered during the ice sheet's fall, valiantly stood against the earthen giant arms swarming towards Emilia, using them as footholds. Some stopped the arms with their bodies, others punched them to divert their course, some were crushed while striking poses as they were overpowered—they showed a variety of actions.

What particularly irritated Aldebaran was the one that took Emilia's hand, helping her escape the demonic hands of the giant arms while throwing her forward to assist her advance.

A convenient total of seven ice Natsuki Subarus fulfilled their roles, shattering, being born anew to support Emilia, only to be shattered and resurrected again, all while maintaining the rule of a maximum of seven. They protected Emilia from the hundreds of approaching rocky demon hands and continued to assist her.

—Magic is something that gives form to thoughts and wishes, the Witch had said.

He had thought it was a rather romantic thing for *that* Witch to say, but considering that turning wind into blades to slice an opponent to pieces or generating crimson flames to burn everything down in rage could also be insisted as manifestations of thoughts and wishes, perhaps it wasn't entirely wrong.

If so, then what kind of wish's manifestation was the scene before Aldebaran's eyes now, where ice soldiers shaped like Natsuki Subaru helped Emilia?

The pit of his stomach churned. Not in a metaphorical sense, but truly, in the literal sense.

Aldebaran channeled that welling-up negative emotion into his next attack.

It was—

"—Sorry for the 'ten-don', but here comes the trump card again."

As Aldebaran spoke, a massive, powerful boulder—large enough to span the entire width of the Agzdad Ravine gap—came plummeting down directly onto the heads of Emilia and the others.

An inescapable crush-killing by immense mass—it was the return of the trap he had set in the royal capital.

**What he wanted—no, what he *needed*, was a single victory in his lifetime.**

It would be no exaggeration to say that Aldebaran's entire life existed solely for that victory.

Everything given to him, poured into him, bestowed upon him since his birth—all of it was a signpost prepared for the sake of obtaining, seizing, winning that victory.

He understood the meaning and the heavy responsibility of it all—or so he thought.

But when the moment came to be tested, the resolve he thought he possessed, the plans he thought were in place, the soul he thought he had honed—all of it proved meaningless.

"—No one can defeat the you I created."

Those words given by the Witch were the guiding principle of Aldebaran's life.

As long as he could believe that, no matter what obstacles stood in his way, he could not be stopped. And the thought that he might ever stop believing in the Witch—he would never say it to her face, never let her hear it, but he had thought it impossible.

So, just as the Witch had said, he had convinced himself that he could not be defeated by anyone.

—The moment that faith was shattered, Aldebaran truly died once.

He had experienced 'Death' so many times that counting them felt absurd.

He *had* thought it absurd to count, but he was told to count, so he did, yet even recording the actual number did nothing to diminish the sheer absurdity he had tasted.

But he thought that the true meaning of 'Death' was what he tasted that day, at that moment.

It was the Witch's fervent wish, the one battle in his entire life he absolutely could not lose, for which she had gone so far as to create Aldebaran.

And yet—

"—At the moment you should not have lost, you lost."

Seeing the Witch, enjoying the aroma of her tea at the familiar white table of the tea party in the familiar grassland, Aldebaran was crushed by despair at the depth of his sin.

The Witch, whose beauty could be called the pinnacle expressed in just two colors, black and white, her form so irritatingly beautiful and completely unblemished—that was the proof this was a world of dreams.

Because the real Witch, having protected Aldebaran who lost the battle he should have won, had been reduced to a pitiful state, having lost all her limbs, merely waiting for death.

Staring blankly at her, Aldebaran vaguely thought of such a terribly obvious thing—that even this Witch had red blood flowing within her.

The heat, the smell, the sensation of that blood… it couldn't possibly be a lie.

If so, then this world, where all of that was lost and the reality that should exist was painted over with fiction, was clearly and undeniably a mass of lies.

"I don't much like the expression 'fiction'. Even knowing it's not strictly the correct word, it inevitably reminds me of a detestable person."

Whether that was the Witch's idea of a joke or she genuinely wanted to say something out of place, Aldebaran never understood the Witch's true feelings until the very end.

He only thought she must hate him. Or perhaps she was disappointed, had completely given up on him.

But—

"It seems my arm is beyond recovery."

The Witch's gaze fell upon Aldebaran's left arm, which had been lost to the shadows from the shoulder.

There was no pain. That, too, was evidence that this was the Witch's dream castle—returning to reality, unimaginable pain would surely scorch his brain. A pain that could not be erased by 'Death', a true pain that would never disappear.

"You lost an arm, and lost a battle you should not have lost. It seems my plan has ended in failure. I can just imagine what my friends will say. —In the end, it will be left to them, who seek to carry on Flügel's will. How vexing."

It was too painful that his defeat had stolen everything from the Witch.

Without seeking anyone's understanding, distancing herself from her fellow Witches and the comrades she traveled with, going that far for the sake of her wish—and Aldebaran had ruined it.

And the most pathetic thing was that, even knowing this, the resolve and passion he had before his defeat showed no sign of returning to his hollowed soul.

Having lost the battle he should not have lost, Aldebaran's spirit was broken.

His very first battle, the one and only one in his life where defeat was unforgivable, had shattered Aldebaran's heart to pieces.

Witnessing the unavoidable death of the Witch, his closest existence, would likely inflict a truly irreparable wound upon Aldebaran.

"—No one can defeat the you I created."

So, when he was told that once more, Aldebaran didn't understand its meaning.

"Now that things have come to this, we can't afford to stand on ceremony. Let's change the victory conditions. We'll leave her to them... we will prevent the secondary damage."

The Witch was wise, and selfish.

Leaving the bewildered Aldebaran, whose mind was too paralyzed by self-recriminating guilt to function properly, she arbitrarily reached a conclusion on her own, trying to move the conversation forward without allowing any rebuttal.

The changed victory conditions, the newly presented goal of preventing secondary damage—but he didn't think he could achieve even that. Because Aldebaran had already failed.

And yet—

"—No one can defeat the you I created."

Aldebaran didn't understand what made the Witch say that.

Without saying much more to the stunned Aldebaran, the Witch stood up, slowly walked over, and faced him directly from the front.

The face of the Witch, which he had seen more than any other, for the longest time—and on it, an expression he was seeing for the first time.

"—The stars were just against us."

He understood it meant it wasn't Aldebaran's fault.

Unable to reach a conclusion about the emotions welling up inside him, the dream castle abruptly came to an end.

It ended as roughly as tearing away the stage of a kamishibai play, and beyond it lay the miserable reality. Instantly, his brain was dominated by pain and a sense of loss, noise and fresh blood eroded his comprehension. The heavens and earth were stained pitch black, enough to lose all sense of time, and Aldebaran's wailing was drowned out by the screams of the crumbling world, not even reaching himself.

Yet, in that world where everything was chaos, amidst noise so loud he couldn't even hear his own voice, he clearly heard it—hoarse, feeble, on the verge of dying out.

The Witch's final incantation, which he didn't want to miss—

"—Al Shamak."

